legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:589:| OD95 | <a id="od95"></a>What `Time`, `Env`, `Process` and `Alloc` mean (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: What authority each of `Time`, `Env`, `Process` and `Alloc` names. Bears on TRUST-D1, TRUST-4. The current compiler: no document states them; HANDLE_CAPABILITIES §2 defined `Console`, `File` and `Network`, and SAFETY.md `Random` (TRUST-D1). std requires `Time` for its clock and sleep functions, `Env` for environment variables and for `std.net`'s `bind` and `connect`, `Process` for exit, spawn, wait and kill, and `Alloc` for heap allocation. Options: state each as std uses it, or narrow or split it (std's `Env` on socket functions is the case to settle). Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-95 | see OPEN-95 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): State each; move socket `bind`/`connect` from `Env` to `Network` |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:591:| OD97 | <a id="od97"></a>A build profile that omits or demands an overflow check (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a build profile may omit the run-time check of an integer operation that no proof discharges, or refuse such an operation at compile time. Bears on ARITH-3, ARITH-9. The current compiler: every profile emits every check, and no option turns one off; ARITH-3's rationale says a profile changes reports, never code generation, and OD10 decided that arithmetic is checked "by default". The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §3.1 instead let a release profile omit a check that is "explicitly configured off and reported", and a provable or high-integrity profile refuse an unproved overflow site. Options: (a) every profile keeps every check that no discharged proof removes; (b) a release profile may turn checks off when configured and reported, never wrapping; (c) as (a), and a profile may also refuse an operation whose no-overflow obligation is not discharged. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-97 | see OPEN-97 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Keep every check; a profile may refuse undischarged ops |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:603:| OD109 | <a id="od109"></a>Specializations that differ only in a function type's capabilities (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether the symbol of a generic function's instantiation includes the capability set of a function-type argument, so that two instantiations that differ only in that set have distinct symbols. Bears on TYPE-17, TYPE-18. The current compiler: one: `pick<T: Copy>` instantiated at `fn(Int) -> Int` and at `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int` emits the single function `pick_for_Fn_T_Int_to_Int_E`, because Mono's `tyToSuffix` drops a function type's capability set while it keeps a struct's capability arguments (E0809's history), and the program runs correctly since capabilities have no run-time representation (TYPE-18). The archived [HANDLE_CAPABILITIES.md](../archive/design-records/HANDLE_CAPABILITIES.md) R8 required collision-free symbol identity across instantiations and left code sharing to its own argument. Options: (a) the set is part of the symbol, as for struct capability arguments; (b) it is not, one symbol serving both, stated as erasure with its correctness argument. Whether two symbols share one body stays an implementation choice (TYPE-18). Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | one specialization: Mono's symbol drops a function type's capability set | TYPE-17, TYPE-18 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Set is part of the symbol |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:618:| OD124 | <a id="od124"></a>Profiles in the specification (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether the profiles (the admission profiles Safe, Predictable and Proof, and the build profiles) belong to the language specification, in a chapter of their own, and whether a profile may change what an accepted program does. Bears on ARITH-9, OPEN-97, PASS-3, PASS-4. The current compiler: a profile only refuses or reports: `--check predictable` reports its five gates (no recursion, bounded loops, no allocation, no extern calls, no `File`, `Network` or `Process`), `[policy] predictable = true` in `Concrete.toml` turns a violation into E0610, and no profile changes the behaviour of a program it accepts; ARITH-9 states that for arithmetic. [PROFILES.md](../platform/PROFILES.md) says a profile never changes what a program means, while OPEN-97 asks whether a build profile may omit a run-time check. Options: (a) no profiles chapter: profiles are tooling, and the spec states only that no profile changes an accepted program's behaviour; (b) a profiles chapter whose entries state each profile's admission rules as refusals, with that same guarantee; (c) as (b), with build profiles allowed to differ as OPEN-97 decides. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-124 | see OPEN-124 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Profiles chapter of refusals; no behaviour change |
